---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
import ConfidenceStrip from "../components/ConfidenceStrip.astro";
import OutcomeMark from "../components/OutcomeMark.astro";
import Rulebook from "../components/Rulebook.astro";
import { specimens, stats, type Kind } from "../lib/data";
import { KIND_PLURAL } from "../lib/describe";
import { dec, n, ratio } from "../lib/format";
import { OUTCOMES } from "../lib/outcomes";

const KINDS: Kind[] = ["issue", "ci", "status", "log", "supercomputer"];
const oom = specimens.find((s) => s.input.kind === "log")!;
const ci = specimens.find((s) => s.input.kind === "ci")!;
const unsure = specimens.find((s) => s.outcome === "unsure")!;
---
<Site
  title="How it works"
  description="Five stages in plain words: one shape for every event, grouping before any model call, one label per problem, a rulebook that decides, and an honest not-sure."
  current="how"
>
  <div class="wrap page">
    <header class="head">
      <h1>How it works</h1>
      <p class="lede">Five stages. Only one of them uses a language model, and it is never the one that decides what happens.</p>
      <ol class="toc">
        <li><a href="#shape">Everything becomes one shape</a></li>
        <li><a href="#group">Same problem, one pile</a></li>
        <li><a href="#label">The model reads each pile once</a></li>
        <li><a href="#rules">A rulebook, not the model, decides</a></li>
        <li><a href="#unsure">When it is not sure, it says so</a></li>
      </ol>
    </header>

    <section id="shape" class="stage">
      <div class="say">
        <h2>Everything becomes one shape</h2>
        <p>A GitHub issue, a failed CI run, a status-page incident and a line from a server log look nothing alike. Each source has a small adapter that turns what it reads into the same envelope: where it came from, when, a title, a body, and a link back to the original. Nothing after this point knows or cares which kind of source it was.</p>
        <p class="gloss"><b>Adapter:</b> a small piece of code per source. Adding a new source means adding one adapter; nothing else changes.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="kinds">
        {KINDS.map((k) => <li><span>{KIND_PLURAL[k]}</span><b>{n(stats.events_by_kind[k])}</b></li>)}
        <li class="total"><span>in one shape</span><b>{n(stats.events)}</b></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="group" class="stage">
      <div class="say">
        <h2>Same problem, one pile</h2>
        <p>Before any model is involved, events that are the same problem are put in one pile. Log lines are grouped by the sentence they share, with the numbers and names that change from line to line set aside. Written reports are grouped when their wording is close, first by shared words and then by meaning.</p>
        <p>The grouping is deterministic: the same input always produces the same piles, run after run. That matters because every later decision is attached to a pile.</p>
        <p class="gloss"><b>Pile:</b> what the code calls a cluster. {n(stats.events)} events became {n(stats.clusters)} piles, {ratio(stats.events, stats.clusters)} fewer things to read.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="example">
        <figcaption>One pile from the Linux server log</figcaption>
        <pre>{oom.input.body}{"\n"}{oom.cluster.siblings.join("\n")}{"\n"}... and the rest of a pile of {n(oom.cluster.size)} lines</pre>
        <p>One pile of <b>{n(oom.cluster.size)}</b> lines, read once.</p>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section id="label" class="stage">
      <div class="say">
        <h2>The model reads each pile once</h2>
        <p>A language model is shown a few samples from each pile and asked four things: what kind of problem it is, how bad, how sure it is, and to say it in one sentence. That is all it returns. It is asked {n(stats.clusters)} times for this corpus, not {n(stats.events)}.</p>
        <p class="gloss"><b>Confidence:</b> the model's own estimate, from 0 to 1, that it named the kind of problem correctly. The rulebook reads it; it never acts on its own.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="example">
        <figcaption>What it returned for the vscode CI failure</figcaption>
        <dl class="verdict">
          <div><dt>Kind of problem</dt><dd>{ci.verdict.category}</dd></div>
          <div><dt>How bad</dt><dd>{ci.verdict.severity}</dd></div>
          <div class="wide"><dt>How sure, {dec(ci.verdict.confidence)}</dt><dd><ConfidenceStrip value={ci.verdict.confidence} /></dd></div>
          <div class="wide"><dt>In one sentence</dt><dd>{ci.verdict.summary}</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section id="rules" class="stage wide-stage">
      <div class="say">
        <h2>A rulebook, not the model, decides</h2>
        <p>The model's answer goes through a short table of rules. They are read top to bottom and the first one that matches decides. A critical problem always goes to a person, however sure the model is: paging someone is cheap, a wrong automatic action during an outage is not.</p>
      </div>
      <Rulebook heading="The whole rulebook" />
      <ul class="ends">
        {OUTCOMES.map((o) => <li><OutcomeMark outcome={o.key} /><b>{n(stats.outcomes[o.key])}</b><span>{o.label}: {o.sentence}</span></li>)}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="unsure" class="stage">
      <div class="say">
        <h2>When it is not sure, it says so</h2>
        <p>{n(stats.outcomes.unsure)} piles ended as "not sure" and {n(stats.outcomes.person)} went to a person. Those are outcomes, not failures: a system that always has an answer is guessing some of the time.</p>
        <p>The clearest example is a vscode issue titled "{unsure.input.title}", with a stack of system information and no description of what went wrong. The model rated it {dec(unsure.verdict.confidence)} sure, below the line where anything happens on its own.</p>
        <p><a href={`/case/${unsure.slug}/`}>Follow that case through</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <nav class="next" aria-label="Next">
      <a href="/measured/"><span>Next</span>How it was measured</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</Site>

<style>
  .page { padding-top: var(--space-7); }
  .head { max-width: 60rem; }
  .lede { margin-top: var(--space-4); font-size: var(--step-1); line-height: 1.5; color: #26332c; max-width: 44ch; }
  .toc { margin-top: var(--space-5); padding-left: var(--space-5); display: grid; gap: var(--space-1); }

  .stage { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.1fr) minmax(0, 0.9fr); gap: var(--space-7); padding-top: var(--space-7); margin-top: var(--space-7); border-top: 2px solid var(--ink); align-items: start; }
  .wide-stage { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--space-5); }
  .say { display: grid; gap: var(--space-4); }
  .say h2 { font-size: var(--step-3); }
  .say p { max-width: var(--measure); color: #26332c; }
  .gloss { padding-left: var(--space-4); border-left: 1px solid var(--ink); font-size: var(--step--1); }

  .kinds { list-style: none; padding: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--ink); }
  .kinds li { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: var(--space-4); padding: var(--space-3) 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .kinds b { font: 500 var(--step-0)/1.4 var(--font-mono); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .kinds .total { border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .kinds .total b { font: 700 var(--step-2)/1 var(--font-display); }

  .example { margin: 0; border: 1px solid var(--ink); }
  .example figcaption { padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink); font: 400 var(--step--1)/1.4 var(--font-mono); color: var(--muted); }
  .example pre { margin: 0; padding: var(--space-4); background: var(--panel); color: var(--panel-ink); font: 400 0.78rem/1.7 var(--font-mono); white-space: pre-wrap; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .example > p { padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4); }
  .verdict { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
  .verdict > div { padding: var(--space-4); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); min-width: 0; }
  .verdict > div:not(.wide) + div:not(.wide) { border-left: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .verdict .wide { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
  .verdict > div:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
  .verdict dt { font: 400 var(--step--2)/1.3 var(--font-mono); color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
  .verdict dd { margin: 0; font-weight: 600; }

  .ends { list-style: none; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--ink); }
  .ends li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: auto 5rem 1fr; gap: var(--space-3); align-items: baseline; padding: var(--space-3) 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .ends b { font: 700 var(--step-1)/1 var(--font-display); }

  .next { margin-top: var(--space-8); border-top: 1px solid var(--ink); padding-top: var(--space-5); text-align: right; }
  .next a { display: inline-grid; gap: var(--space-1); text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; font-size: var(--step-1); }
  .next span { font: 400 var(--step--1)/1.3 var(--font-mono); color: var(--muted); }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .stage { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--space-5); }
  }
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .ends li { grid-template-columns: auto 3.5rem 1fr; }
    .verdict { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .verdict > div:not(.wide) + div:not(.wide) { border-left: 0; }
  }
</style>
